WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:07.471
Real-time systems need resource controls
because connections and messages can be

2
00:00:07.551 --> 00:00:11.436
long-lived, frequent, and highly
connected.

3
00:00:11.516 --> 00:00:17.591
A SignalR application may handle chat
updates, dashboards, telemetry,

4
00:00:17.671 --> 00:00:24.841
collaboration events, notifications,
streaming data, or administrative signals.

5
00:00:24.921 --> 00:00:30.996
Each pattern has different pressure on
CPU, memory, network bandwidth,

6
00:00:31.076 --> 00:00:34.960
queues, databases, and downstream
services.

7
00:00:35.040 --> 00:00:37.132
Start with message size.

8
00:00:37.212 --> 00:00:44.183
The expected size of incoming messages
should be reviewed and constrained to match

9
00:00:44.263 --> 00:00:45.359
the feature.

10
00:00:45.439 --> 00:00:51.912
Large messages may affect server memory,
serialization cost, logs, queues,

11
00:00:51.992 --> 00:00:53.088
and clients.

12
00:00:53.168 --> 00:00:58.844
Streaming and upload-like patterns need
especially clear limits,

13
00:00:58.924 --> 00:01:05.498
because they can behave differently from
ordinary one-message method calls.

14
00:01:05.578 --> 00:01:08.466
Frequency matters as much as size.

15
00:01:08.546 --> 00:01:15.019
Typing indicators, cursor movement,
location updates, telemetry streams,

16
00:01:15.099 --> 00:01:22.270
dashboard refreshes, and notification
bursts can be individually small while still

17
00:01:22.350 --> 00:01:25.238
creating heavy backend pressure.

18
00:01:25.318 --> 00:01:31.493
Defensive design uses bounded queues,
controlled fan-out, throttling,

19
00:01:31.573 --> 00:01:38.942
batching, sampling, and backpressure
thinking so the system has predictable behavior

20
00:01:39.022 --> 00:01:40.218
under stress.

21
00:01:40.298 --> 00:01:43.883
Reconnect behavior also deserves review.

22
00:01:43.963 --> 00:01:51.034
Automatic reconnect and stateful reconnect
features can improve user experience,

23
00:01:51.114 --> 00:01:57.687
but buffered data and resumed delivery
should remain within intended limits.

24
00:01:57.767 --> 00:02:02.747
The application should define what state
can be recovered,

25
00:02:02.827 --> 00:02:09.300
how long it is retained, and what happens
when the client falls too far behind.

26
00:02:09.380 --> 00:02:13.463
Deployment architecture can change the
answer.

27
00:02:13.543 --> 00:02:20.017
Azure SignalR Service, Redis backplanes,
reverse proxies, load balancers,

28
00:02:20.097 --> 00:02:26.271
container platforms, and scale-out
settings affect capacity, routing,

29
00:02:26.351 --> 00:02:30.435
sticky behavior, timeouts, and
observability.

30
00:02:30.515 --> 00:02:33.801
Resource controls are not one setting;

31
00:02:33.881 --> 00:02:40.454
they are a set of design decisions that
keep real-time features useful without

32
00:02:40.534 --> 00:02:44.020
letting them overwhelm the application.
